WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
Choosing what to run, with evidence.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:08.195
A working engineer picks a local model the same way as an API model, with two extra constraints.

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:21.211
Start from the hardware: subtract a safety margin for the operating system and other apps from your memory, then use your estimator to list which model sizes and quantizations fit at the context length you need.

4
00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:27.955
Shortlist two or three models whose licenses fit (lesson 1) and whose cards suit the task (lesson 2).

5
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:40.565
Try the shortlisted quantizations on a labeled sample: if Q4_K_M matches Q8_0 on your task, take the smaller one; if it doesn’t, the extra memory is the price of accuracy.

6
00:00:40.845 --> 00:00:45.880
Measure time to first token, decode speed, and seconds per task on warm runs.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:56.677
Finally, run the same sample through a frontier API, so you know exactly what local inference costs you in quality and what it saves you in privacy, money, or latency.

8
00:00:56.957 --> 00:01:01.480
That comparison is the core of this studio, and the lab is your first version of it.
